.TH SIN 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
sin, sinf, sinl, cos, cosf, cosl, tan, tanf, tanl, sincos, sincosf, sincosl \- trigonometric functions
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double sin(double " x );
.br
.BI "float sinf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double sinl(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double cos(double " x );
.br
.BI "float cosf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double cosl(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double tan(double " x );
.br
.BI "float tanf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double tanl(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "void sincos(double " x ", double *" sinp ", double *" cosp );
.br
.BI "void sincosf(float " x ", float *" sinp ", float *" cosp );
.br
.BI "void sincosl(long double " x ", long double *" sinp ", long double *" cosp );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR sin (),
.BR cos (),
and
.BR tan ()
functions return the sine, cosine, and tangent of
.IR x ,
respectively, where
.I x
is expressed in radians.
.PP
The
.BR sinf (),
.BR cosf (),
and
.BR tanf ()
variants take and return
.B float
values.  The
.BR sinl (),
.BR cosl (),
and
.BR tanl ()
variants take and return
.B "long double"
values.
.PP
The
.BR sincos ()
function computes both
.BI sin( x )
and
.BI cos( x )
in a single call, storing the sine in
.RI * sinp
and the cosine in
.RI * cosp .
On platforms whose hardware computes both values together, this is
faster than calling
.BR sin ()
and
.BR cos ()
separately.  The
.BR sincosf ()
and
.BR sincosl ()
variants operate on
.B float
and
.B "long double"
respectively.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR sin (),
.BR cos (),
and their variants return a value in the range
.BR [\-1,\ +1] .
.PP
.BR tan ()
and its variants return the tangent of
.IR x ,
which can be any finite value or, near odd multiples of
.IR \(*p/2 ,
a value of arbitrarily large magnitude.
.PP
If
.I x
is a NaN, all functions return a NaN.
.PP
If
.I x
is positive or negative infinity, all functions return a NaN and a
domain error occurs.
.PP
.BR sincos ()
and its variants do not return a value; they store their results
through
.I sinp
and
.IR cosp .
.SH ERRORS
See
.BR math_error (7)
for information on how to determine whether an error has occurred when
calling these functions.
.PP
The following errors can occur:
.TP
Domain error: \fIx\fR is an infinity
An invalid floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_INVALID )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR EDOM .
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR sin (),
.BR sinf (),
.BR sinl (),
.BR cos (),
.BR cosf (),
.BR cosl (),
.BR tan (),
.BR tanf (),
.BR tanl (),
.BR sincos (),
.BR sincosf (),
.BR sincosl ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR sin (),
.BR cos (),
.BR tan ()
and their
.BR f / l
variants conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.
.PP
.BR sincos (),
.BR sincosf (),
and
.BR sincosl ()
are GNU extensions, also adopted by FreeBSD.  They are not specified
by any standard.
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program computes \fIsin(\(*p/4)\fR and prints the result:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double x = M_PI / 4;

    printf("sin(%.6f) = %.6f\en", x, sin(x));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
The next example uses
.BR sincos ()
to compute both values in a single call:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double x = M_PI / 6;
    double s, c;

    sincos(x, &s, &c);
    printf("sin(%.6f) = %.6f, cos(%.6f) = %.6f\en",
           x, s, x, c);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
